Here is a C++ function that takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <regex>
#include <string>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html = markdown;
    std::regex re(R"(#\s*([a-zA-Z0-9_]+)\s*([a-zA-Z0-9_]+)");
    html = std::regex_replace(html, re, "<h1>$1</h1>");
    return html;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "# 1\n# 2\n# 3\n# 4\n# 5\n# 6\n# 7";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function uses a regular expression to match the markdown headings (`# 1`, `# 2`, etc.) and replaces them with corresponding HTML headings (`<h1>`, `<h2>`, etc.). The `std::regex_replace` function is used to perform the replacement. The `main` function demonstrates how to use this function and prints the resulting HTML formatted text.

